\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces The intensity stabilisation concept involves two loops. The inner loop is responsible for stabilising the laser so that the IMC can lock. The outer loop is used to stabilise the laser for observation mode. The two loops sense the laser intensity noise at different points. The inner loop uses a low power beam transmitted by the PMC. The outer loop uses a pick-off from the main beam. An AOM will be used to actuate on the laser's power.\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.41}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterissconcept}{{3.11}{30}{The intensity stabilisation concept involves two loops. The inner loop is responsible for stabilising the laser so that the IMC can lock. The outer loop is used to stabilise the laser for observation mode. The two loops sense the laser intensity noise at different points. The inner loop uses a low power beam transmitted by the PMC. The outer loop uses a pick-off from the main beam. An AOM will be used to actuate on the laser's power.\relax }{figure.caption.41}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces A reproduction of the intensity noise requirement from \cite  {PSL_requirements}. The outer loop requirement is shown in blue and the inner loop is shown by the orange dashed line. At frequencies below 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the outer loop requirement is determined by the radiation pressure noise that is expected to arise due to imbalances in the arm power. The requirement is most stringent at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. Due to the PRC cavity pole, the requirement relaxes above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. The inner loop requirement is less extreme than the outer loop as it is only used to stabilise the laser in the initial locking sequence and not during an observation run.\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.42}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterissrequirement}{{3.12}{31}{A reproduction of the intensity noise requirement from \cite {PSL_requirements}. The outer loop requirement is shown in blue and the inner loop is shown by the orange dashed line. At frequencies below 10\,Hz, the outer loop requirement is determined by the radiation pressure noise that is expected to arise due to imbalances in the arm power. The requirement is most stringent at 10\,Hz. Due to the PRC cavity pole, the requirement relaxes above 10\,Hz. The inner loop requirement is less extreme than the outer loop as it is only used to stabilise the laser in the initial locking sequence and not during an observation run.\relax }{figure.caption.42}{}}
