\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces The free running noise of the NPRO and each amplifier is shown. The noise after the PMC was also measured. There is a forest of 4\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz lines due to the Beckhoff control computer in the amplifier and PMC noise curves. Due to the non linear nature of the saturated amplifiers, at high frequencies the output beam intensity noise decreases relative to the input beam. The PMC couples length noise to intensity noise between 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz and 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz. This excess can be tuned away in the PDH servo.\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.45}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrn}{{3.13}{33}{The free running noise of the NPRO and each amplifier is shown. The noise after the PMC was also measured. There is a forest of 4\,Hz lines due to the Beckhoff control computer in the amplifier and PMC noise curves. Due to the non linear nature of the saturated amplifiers, at high frequencies the output beam intensity noise decreases relative to the input beam. The PMC couples length noise to intensity noise between 100\,Hz and 10\,kHz. This excess can be tuned away in the PDH servo.\relax }{figure.caption.45}{}}
