\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces A sketch of how the AOM transfer function was measured. The components of the intensity stabilisation servo has also been shown. The PMC was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. An IC (AD734) that performs a square root operation to an input signal was used to linearise the AOM's affect on beam power to an input signal. The transfer function was measured between the input to the lineariser and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation. The servo electronics are detailed in D1001985 and D1001998.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.47}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}{{3.14}{35}{A sketch of how the AOM transfer function was measured. The components of the intensity stabilisation servo has also been shown. The PMC was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. An IC (AD734) that performs a square root operation to an input signal was used to linearise the AOM's affect on beam power to an input signal. The transfer function was measured between the input to the lineariser and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation. The servo electronics are detailed in D1001985 and D1001998.\relax }{figure.caption.47}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces Using the layout shown in figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the input to the linearisation stage before the AOM to the light power change on the ISS photodiode was measured. The response was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. The transfer function is flat over the region where there open loop gain needs to be greater than one ($\sim 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\kilo \hertz }$), There is a small amount of phase lag at higher frequencies.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.48}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomtransferfunction}{{3.15}{36}{Using the layout shown in figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the input to the linearisation stage before the AOM to the light power change on the ISS photodiode was measured. The response was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. The transfer function is flat over the region where there open loop gain needs to be greater than one ($\sim 10\,\si {\kilo \hertz }$), There is a small amount of phase lag at higher frequencies.\relax }{figure.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{37}{section*.51}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.5.2.3}Reasoning for Using the AOM Rather than the Current Shunt}{37}{subsubsection.3.5.2.3}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces A sketch of how the current shunt transfer function was measured. The PMC was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. The current of one of the pump diodes in the second amplifier was modulated by applying a signal to a current shunt. The transfer function was measured between the input to the current shunt electronics and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.52}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptercstfmeasurement}{{3.16}{38}{A sketch of how the current shunt transfer function was measured. The PMC was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. The current of one of the pump diodes in the second amplifier was modulated by applying a signal to a current shunt. The transfer function was measured between the input to the current shunt electronics and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation.\relax }{figure.caption.52}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Using the layout shown in Figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchaptercstfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the current shunt input electronics and the intensity stabilisation photodiode was measured. The transfer function was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. At 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, where the noise requirement is hardest to meet, the gain of transfer function changes significantly depending on the pump diode DC current. The phase at this frequency does not matter, as it is not near the unity gain point of the servo.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.53}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptercurrentshunttf}{{3.17}{39}{Using the layout shown in Figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchaptercstfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the current shunt input electronics and the intensity stabilisation photodiode was measured. The transfer function was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. At 10\,Hz, where the noise requirement is hardest to meet, the gain of transfer function changes significantly depending on the pump diode DC current. The phase at this frequency does not matter, as it is not near the unity gain point of the servo.\relax }{figure.caption.53}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.5.3}Open Loop Transfer Function of the Intensity Stabilisation Servo}{40}{subsection.3.5.3}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{40}{section*.54}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{40}{section*.55}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.18}{\ignorespaces The open loop transfer function of the servo was measured by injecting a signal at a times one adding stage. The points immediately before and after this sate were measured, and the ratio of these two stages gives the open loop transfer function of the servo.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.56}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteroltfmeasurement}{{3.18}{41}{The open loop transfer function of the servo was measured by injecting a signal at a times one adding stage. The points immediately before and after this sate were measured, and the ratio of these two stages gives the open loop transfer function of the servo.\relax }{figure.caption.56}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces The open loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in Figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapteroltfmeasurement}. The unity gain point is at 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\kilo \hertz } with 70\si {\degree } of phase margin. The shaping of the servo is $1/f$ at the unity gain point, but to achieve the higher gain required at low frequency, the servo has a $1/f^2$ slope below the unity gain point. The measurement saturated below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.57}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteropenlooptransferfunction}{{3.19}{42}{The open loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in Figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchapteroltfmeasurement}. The unity gain point is at 48\,\si {\kilo \hertz } with 70\si {\degree } of phase margin. The shaping of the servo is $1/f$ at the unity gain point, but to achieve the higher gain required at low frequency, the servo has a $1/f^2$ slope below the unity gain point. The measurement saturated below 100\,Hz.\relax }{figure.caption.57}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.5.4}Measurement of the Intensity Stabilisation of a 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt } Beam to the Inner Loop Requirement}{43}{subsection.3.5.4}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces The Free running noise of the laser is shown in red. The noise of the light transmitted by the PMC is shown in blue; this meets the requirement that is shown by the dashed black line. The noise level is determined by the in loop noise, shown in green, the shot noise and other technical noise sources. The sum of these is shown by the dotted brown line. Below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}H\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em} the noise is shot noise limited. There is a discrepancy between the noise budget and the measured noise. This is likely due to dust falling through the beam or scattered light. Near the unity gain point of the servo, the noise is enhanced by the servo due to gain peaking.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.58}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomstabilisationnoise}{{3.20}{44}{The Free running noise of the laser is shown in red. The noise of the light transmitted by the PMC is shown in blue; this meets the requirement that is shown by the dashed black line. The noise level is determined by the in loop noise, shown in green, the shot noise and other technical noise sources. The sum of these is shown by the dotted brown line. Below 100\,H\, the noise is shot noise limited. There is a discrepancy between the noise budget and the measured noise. This is likely due to dust falling through the beam or scattered light. Near the unity gain point of the servo, the noise is enhanced by the servo due to gain peaking.\relax }{figure.caption.58}{}}
