\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.7.1}Modelling the Open Loop Transfer Function for Locking the PMC}{48}{subsection.3.7.1}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.22}{\ignorespaces A sketch of the frequency stabilisation servo. An EOM creates 35\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}MHz sidebands which are used to produce a PDH locking signal. A PZT controls the lenght of the cavity at high frequencies. A heater and a digital loop is used to provide slow control that corrects for frequency drifts associated with change in the NPRO temperature. This is required as otherwise the PZT would run out of range. The open loop transfer function was measured using signals at A and B. The frequency noise was measured using the error point and control signals marked by $c$ and $d$, with this notation corresponding with the notation in Figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementmathssketch} and Equation \ref  {eq:ligolaserchaptermeasurement_of_frequency_noise}.\relax }}{49}{figure.caption.64}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementsketch}{{3.22}{49}{A sketch of the frequency stabilisation servo. An EOM creates 35\,MHz sidebands which are used to produce a PDH locking signal. A PZT controls the lenght of the cavity at high frequencies. A heater and a digital loop is used to provide slow control that corrects for frequency drifts associated with change in the NPRO temperature. This is required as otherwise the PZT would run out of range. The open loop transfer function was measured using signals at A and B. The frequency noise was measured using the error point and control signals marked by $c$ and $d$, with this notation corresponding with the notation in Figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementmathssketch} and Equation \ref {eq:ligolaserchaptermeasurement_of_frequency_noise}.\relax }{figure.caption.64}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.23}{\ignorespaces The servo can be considered as being made of three parts: the optics, the shaping electronics and the PZT. The optics block is obtained after the photodiode signal is demodulated. When at resonance and below the cavity pole, this is a flat transfer function that is proportional to the difference in frequency. Since this cavity pole is in the \tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}MHz region and this measurement will only go to the kHz region, the cavity pole can be ignored. The electronics transfer function includes everything after the demodulator up to and including the final high power OP amp. The PZT transfer function converts a voltage to a cavity frequency. The PZT acts as a low pass filter due to it having capacitance.\relax }}{50}{figure.caption.66}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementmathssketch}{{3.23}{50}{The servo can be considered as being made of three parts: the optics, the shaping electronics and the PZT. The optics block is obtained after the photodiode signal is demodulated. When at resonance and below the cavity pole, this is a flat transfer function that is proportional to the difference in frequency. Since this cavity pole is in the \,MHz region and this measurement will only go to the kHz region, the cavity pole can be ignored. The electronics transfer function includes everything after the demodulator up to and including the final high power OP amp. The PZT transfer function converts a voltage to a cavity frequency. The PZT acts as a low pass filter due to it having capacitance.\relax }{figure.caption.66}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{51}{section*.68}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{}{51}{section*.70}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.24}{\ignorespaces A ramp was applied to the PZT and photodiode signal was measured before and after demodulation. The demodulated photodiode signal is shown in blue. The photodiode signal is shown in red. Around the peak of the photodiode signal the slope of the demodulated signal gives the gain of the `optics' transfer function. This can be expressed in units of MHz/V by converting the time axis to a frequency axis. This is done by using the FWHM of the photodiode signal as a reference frequency. The level of the FWHM is indicated by the dashed red line.\relax }}{52}{figure.caption.67}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptermixercalibration}{{3.24}{52}{A ramp was applied to the PZT and photodiode signal was measured before and after demodulation. The demodulated photodiode signal is shown in blue. The photodiode signal is shown in red. Around the peak of the photodiode signal the slope of the demodulated signal gives the gain of the `optics' transfer function. This can be expressed in units of MHz/V by converting the time axis to a frequency axis. This is done by using the FWHM of the photodiode signal as a reference frequency. The level of the FWHM is indicated by the dashed red line.\relax }{figure.caption.67}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.25}{\ignorespaces A ramp was applied to the PZT and the reflected light from the cavity was measured. The FSR of the cavity is known, so the the time in this measurement can be converted to frequency giving the PZT transfer function at low frequencies. The ramp signal is shown in blue and the reflected light is shown in red.\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.69}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterpmcscan}{{3.25}{53}{A ramp was applied to the PZT and the reflected light from the cavity was measured. The FSR of the cavity is known, so the the time in this measurement can be converted to frequency giving the PZT transfer function at low frequencies. The ramp signal is shown in blue and the reflected light is shown in red.\relax }{figure.caption.69}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.26}{\ignorespaces The open loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in figure \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementsketch}. This is shown in red. The electronics transfer function without the `boost' provided by an extra integrator stage was measured and is shown in blue. The model of this is shown in orange. The boosted electronics model is shown in purple. The green line shows the expected servo gain based on the bossted electronics model and the measurements shown in Figures \ref  {fig:ligolaserchaptermixercalibration} and \ref  {fig:ligolaserchapterpmcscan}. This model is within 2\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}dB of the measurement. The unity gain point was measured to be at $\sim 800$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz.\relax }}{54}{figure.caption.71}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterpmcloopmodeltf}{{3.26}{54}{The open loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in figure \ref {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementsketch}. This is shown in red. The electronics transfer function without the `boost' provided by an extra integrator stage was measured and is shown in blue. The model of this is shown in orange. The boosted electronics model is shown in purple. The green line shows the expected servo gain based on the bossted electronics model and the measurements shown in Figures \ref {fig:ligolaserchaptermixercalibration} and \ref {fig:ligolaserchapterpmcscan}. This model is within 2\,dB of the measurement. The unity gain point was measured to be at $\sim 800$\,Hz.\relax }{figure.caption.71}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.7.2}Frequency Noise Measurement}{55}{subsection.3.7.2}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.27}{\ignorespaces The mixer signal combined with the open loop transfer function is shown in red. This is equivalent to the frequency noise of the system. The PZT signal should agree with this measurement below the unity gain point ($\sim 800$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz). A signal was applied to the PZT at 1111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. This is below the corner frequency of the PZT and above the unity gain point for the servo. This should match with the mixer signal There is a factor of two between these two measurements. This can be attributed to imperfect calibration due to nominal parameters of the PMC being used. The typical noise expected from an NPRO is shown by the dashed black line. Between 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz and 1000\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, there is a bulge in the frequency noise. This is likely due to acoustically driven length noise of the PMC as the PMC is not vacuum sealed.\relax }}{56}{figure.caption.72}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoise}{{3.27}{56}{The mixer signal combined with the open loop transfer function is shown in red. This is equivalent to the frequency noise of the system. The PZT signal should agree with this measurement below the unity gain point ($\sim 800$\,Hz). A signal was applied to the PZT at 1111\,Hz. This is below the corner frequency of the PZT and above the unity gain point for the servo. This should match with the mixer signal There is a factor of two between these two measurements. This can be attributed to imperfect calibration due to nominal parameters of the PMC being used. The typical noise expected from an NPRO is shown by the dashed black line. Between 100\,Hz and 1000\,Hz, there is a bulge in the frequency noise. This is likely due to acoustically driven length noise of the PMC as the PMC is not vacuum sealed.\relax }{figure.caption.72}{}}
